## Summary

I made the unified-log module idiomatic in three ways. **First**, the `UnifiedLogging` trait's sixteen methods dropped their `log_` prefix (`log_quic_pdu` → `quic_pdu`, `log_packet` → `packet`, …) — the trait name already conveys "logging", so the prefix was C-derived noise. **Second**, I converted every free function in the dispatch layer into an inherent method on its first-argument type: the three context-level dispatchers became `Quic::log_app_message` / `Quic::log_pdu` / `Quic::close_logs`, and the thirteen connection-level dispatchers became methods on `Cnx`. The `log_context_free_app_message` → `Quic::log_app_message` and `log_quic_pdu` → `Quic::log_pdu` renames work because the receiver type now disambiguates the context-level variant from the connection-level one. I also removed the duplicate `Cnx::log_app_message` stub that lived in `lib.rs` so unified_log.rs is now the canonical home. **Third**, smaller cleanups: dropped the unused `_raw_data: &[u8]` parameter from the dropped-packet wrapper (the C `UNUSED(raw_data)` never plumbs it through), removed the `#![allow(non_camel_case_types)]` lint suppression (the imported snake_case types from `internal` don't trigger it on use), and rewrote doc-comments to lead with what the Rust API does and keep `C: …` only as a trailing traceability note.


